Yes. Almost everything becomes a question of confidence relative to alternatives, evidence quality, and the cost of being wrong.

You rarely get absolute certainty. What you get is something more operational:

hypothesis → evidence → competing explanations → confidence update → action threshold

And different domains tolerate different thresholds. You can act on 60% confidence when the downside is trivial and the test is cheap. You may want 99.9% when the consequence is catastrophic.

So the mature version is not “believe or disbelieve.” It is:

How confident am I, compared with what, based on which evidence, and is that confidence sufficient for this decision?

That also fits your mirativity/trajectory bundle nicely. An insight can be strongly believed, historically unresolved, independently derived, and still provisional. Confidence is another coordinate, not the whole verdict.